// A string the machine cannot hold. Until v0.1.274 the four backends gave four
// answers to this, and the two most used gave a NUMBER:
//
//   interp  Fatal error: exception Out of memory   exit 2  (OCaml's words)
//   C       -1530494976                            exit 0
//   LLVM    2764472320                             exit 0
//   Wasm    (no output at all)                     exit 1
//
// C and LLVM printed a plausible length and carried on. Two things had to be
// true for that: the count reached the runtime through an `int`, so a 64-bit
// Mere value arrived as its low 32 bits and asked for a string a program could
// actually have -- a DIFFERENT string, silently -- and the allocator never read
// what malloc answered, so a request that did get through died by writing to
// the null it returned.
//
// Both are fixed, and the failure has one name on all four. The size is absurd
// on purpose: 10^15 bytes is refused by every allocator instantly, so this case
// costs nothing and cannot make a machine swap.

let s = str_repeat "ab" 500000000000000;
let _ = print (str_of_int (str_len s));
0
